      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Toronto Blue Jays' season has fractured into a tale of two games, leaving them in a precarious 42-47 spot, third in the AL East and 11.5 games behind the division lead. Just 24 hours ago, the team delivered a stunning <strong>2-0 blanking of the Mariners</strong>, powered by Dylan Cease's seven scoreless innings and nine strikeouts, a franchise-record performance that silenced Seattle's offense. Yet that triumph was instantly overshadowed by an <strong>11-0 humiliation</strong> in the same series, where Logan Gilbert allowed just one hit and Randy Arozarena capped the rout with a grand slam. The contrast is brutal: Cease is rewriting the record book while the bullpen implodes under pressure. Vladimir Guerrero Jr., despite being named an All-Star, has turned down the invite, signaling a team focused on the grind over the glamour. <strong>Tonight at 5:00 PM ET, the Jays face Mariners again in a critical Sunday showdown that could define their July trajectory.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated July 5, 2026"
      },
